# frontend/.env.example
#
# Environment variable template for the React frontend.
# Copy this file to .env and fill in the values for your environment.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Never commit .env to version control.
# This file (.env.example) should always be committed.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# IMPORTANT — Vite exposes only variables prefixed with VITE_ to the browser.
# Do not put secrets in this file. Everything here is visible in the
# compiled JavaScript bundle.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── API ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Base URL of the backend API including /api path suffix.
#
# Local development:
VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:5000/api
#
# Production (replace with your deployed backend URL):
# VITE_API_URL=https://your-api-domain.com/api
#
# If deploying backend to Railway:
# VITE_API_URL=https://your-project.up.railway.app/api
#
# If deploying backend to Render:
# VITE_API_URL=https://your-service.onrender.com/api

# ── Build notes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Before running a production build:
#   1. Set VITE_API_URL to your deployed backend URL
#   2. Run: npm run build
#   3. Deploy the generated dist/ folder to Vercel, Netlify, or Cloudflare Pages
#
# The dist/ folder is a static site — no Node.js required to serve it.